    "story": [
      "the drawing is a timeline: three horizontal lanes (Tab A, Tab B, Auth server) and time runs left to right, so a box further right happens later (said aloud, and the 'time' arrow at the bottom points right)",
      "which lane a box sits on says who does it: there are two boxes both labelled 'read RT1' and two labelled 'POST /refresh (RT1)', one in Tab A's lane and one in Tab B's; only their vertical position tells them apart",
      "Tab B reads RT1 slightly after Tab A, and both read it before Tab A has stored RT2",
      "they first drew Tab B's POST before Tab A's, then moved it to the right so it lands after A's request (A's is already being handled)",
      "the red dashed vertical line labelled 'breaks here' marks the moment it breaks: when Tab B's refresh with the old RT1 reaches the server, after the rotation",
      "the freehand circle on the left is around both 'read RT1' boxes: the window where both tabs hold the same RT1 (said aloud: 'that's the window')",
      "the red box 'RT1 reused → revoke all' is the failure; red was not given a legend, but it is the box they said logs everyone out",
      "'next call 401?' is dashed and has a question mark: a guess that Tab A's next call also fails because RT2 was in the same family",
      "the underline under 'RT1 reused → revoke all' (and the typed note) mark what they are unsure of: revoke the whole family, or just reject the second request",
      "they do not know whether a cross-tab lock (e.g. a broadcast channel) was ever built"
    ],
    "partner_should_ask_about": [
      "does the server revoke the whole token family on reuse, or only reject the second request",
      "is there any cross-tab lock or shared refresh (broadcast channel, storage event) today",
      "what does Tab A do after storing RT2: does its next call really fail (the dashed box)"
    ]
